# THE MAN IN THIS BEAT IS GUARD 2 / GUARD B -- light sandy hair, cream
# short-sleeve shirt, white sash over one shoulder, broad dark-brown wrap skirt.
# He is the one who carries the bark board, read off the board itself: beat 10's
# stage direction is "Guard 2 flips the clipboard around".
#
# THE PROMPT, verbatim from pipeline/wave-drafts.yaml beats.6.authored_b06_cast_0817
# (repo sha b75415098678b47cfbc3a8670de2c9982fcef01cbb180d2253da160004a4b94d; the
# harness copy was hashed on the box with certutil after the sync and matches):
#
#   A guard man with light sandy hair, cream shirt, white shoulder sash and brown
#   wrap skirt turns over a bark board in both hands at reading height, looking
#   down, tall grass, hedgerow behind, pale sky above, cinematic lighting,
#   masterpiece, best quality, very aesthetic No girl, no child, no armor, no
#   helmet, no knight, no white background, no plain background, no dark, no
#   night. No photorealism, no 3D render look. 9:16 vertical, no text.
#
# TOKENS. 72 of 77 positive, 65 of 77 negative, measured on the string that
# actually gets sent with sd_prompt.negative_tokens -- not with compress()'s
# prose estimator, which under-counts a tag list by up to 10 tokens and will
# report headroom while CLIP has already dropped the style anchor. NO TRADE ON
# THIS BEAT: the style tail is byte-identical to the one beats 05, 06 and 09
# already carry, so nothing was cut to make room and the tail is not a variable.
#
# THE LAWS APPLIED. PROP OWNERSHIP: a prop does not bind to a person clause the
# way hair and garments do -- it goes to whoever is drawn nearest -- so the board
# sits INSIDE guard B's own clause, and this beat holds only one man for it to
# land on, which is exactly why it is the beat to establish the grammar on. THE
# HAND LAW: `in both hands` specifies both of the only hands in frame, because an
# unspecified hand is a vacancy the model fills and the negative does not reach
# it. THE VACANCY LAW ABOVE THE HORIZON: `pale sky above` gives the upper third
# its own positive noun; `horizon` is negated nowhere.
#
# WHAT IS LIKELIEST TO FAIL, SAID BEFORE THE RENDER. The bark board is a prop
# four wording attempts and twenty-three inpaint samples failed on, and the frame
# that finally passed (ep2-b06-barkboard-inpaint-0815 s10) got there by
# compositing procedural bark relief and inpainting at strength 0.30 -- NOT by
# wording. So the board's TEXTURE term below is the term this job is most likely
# to lose, and losing it does not touch the cast terms: cast and board are scored
# SEPARATELY so a cast-correct man with a papery board is recorded as what it is
# rather than as a failed plate.
#
# NO plate_ack, no drafts_ack, no gate. $0, local card, no provider, no spend.
